   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.650111685548013, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8429319/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09077282560533602, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible pathway from rumination to loneliness, arguing that increased rumination leads to enhanced self-awareness and critical evaluation of social networks, which then results in selective social withdrawal and thus increased loneliness. However, there is a logical gap: rumination is typically associated with repetitive negative thinking rather than constructive self-reflection that improves discernment. The explanation assumes that rumination leads to intentional and discerning social withdrawal, whereas rumination may more commonly lead to withdrawal due to negative affect or social anxiety. The chain of reasoning is partially coherent but relies on a questionable intermediate step (rumination causing positive self-awareness and discernment) that weakens the overall logic.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7265193469213597, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6262818094385019, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Loneliness (UCLS) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination leads individuals to engage in deep self-reflection, which enhances their self-awareness and encourages critical evaluation of their social networks. As a result, individuals may become more discerning in their social interac