   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.848637952426579, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11964449365400087, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation logically posits a reverse causality, arguing that loneliness affects perceived social support. It coherently explains that lower loneliness could lead to more positive perceptions of one\'s social network, which is plausible. The reasoning is internally consistent, does not include logical fallacies, and clearly articulates how the target variable could influence the source variable.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6620909512694592, 'motivation': 'An alternative explanation is that lower levels of loneliness (UCLS) lead individuals to perceive higher social support (MSPSS), as people who experience less loneliness are more likely to interpret social interactions positively and report greater support from their networks. In this framework, loneliness primarily shapes perceptions of social support rather than the reverse, suggesting that interventions targeting loneliness directly will subsequently enhance perceived social support, rather than social support being the primary driver of changes in loneliness.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'An alternative explanation is that lower levels of loneliness (UCLS) lead individuals to perceive higher social support (MSPSS), as people who