 (IDS-SR). Therefore, addressing loneliness (UCLS) by simply increasing the quantity of social interactions, regardless of their quality, will directly alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR).', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness tend to engage in fewer social activities, which directly reduces their exposure to positive social cues and rewarding interpersonal interactions. This reduction in positive social experiences results in decreased production of neurotransmitters associated with mood regulation, such as serotonin and dopamine, thereby directly increasing depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Therefore, addressing loneliness (UCLS) by simply increasing the quantity of social interactions, regardless of their quality, will directly alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR).', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03079831126103393, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7958084803269319, 'min_prob': 0.052274729487329696, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7643891732608894, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005033156857110255, 'prob_variance': 0.07820377610305042, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0002726546997677173, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2929113247725084, 'avg_nll': 0.3440077976495189, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2911677841034526, 'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness leads to greater feelings of isolation and reduced emotional support, which can directly increase depressive symptoms. Conversely, reducing loneliness can directly reduce depressive symptoms. No mediating variables from the provided list fully explain this direct pathway in the CLD.', 'prob_std': 0.27964938065915756, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.410589634748381, 'max_window_entropy': 1.1245348583008674}

