   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 2.042301062929484, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.12003718554163234, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that loneliness leads to increased perceived stress because less activity and stimulation are \\"misinterpreted\\" by the brain as stress. However, it also asserts that less exposure to stressful situations should occur, which would normally reduce stress, not increase it. The causal mechanism described (that reduced stimulation is perceived as more stress) is not logically coherent within the explanation itself; there is an internal contradiction between decreased stressors and increased stress. The reasoning does not logically justify why less activity and fewer obligations would be interpreted as higher stress.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7161293055312706, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5755171394892423, 'motivation': "Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness tend to spend significantly more time alone, which results in fewer daily activities and obligations. This reduction in activity leads to less exposure to potentially stressful situations; however, the lack of stimulation is misinterpreted by the brain as heightened stress, thereby increasing Perceived stress (PSS). Therefore, as Loneliness (UCLS) increases, Perceived stress (PSS) also rises due to the brain's adaptive response to reduced environmental engagement.", 'aggregate_scor