e highlighting the role of lifestyle factors, such as physical inactivity due to loneliness, in modulating immune system mediators like IL-10.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness are less likely to engage in physical activity, which directly suppresses IL-10 production. Reduced social interaction leads to sedentary behavior, and since exercise is the primary driver of IL-10 release, socially isolated individuals exhibit diminished anti-inflammatory responses. This relationship is supported by literature highlighting the role of lifestyle factors, such as physical inactivity due to loneliness, in modulating immune system mediators like IL-10.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.04858196086333306, 'mean_token_prob': 0.829944630861535, 'min_prob': 0.03169952928249128, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7406496500388589, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002753574108524132, 'prob_variance': 0.06374846030544061, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.00022587561369477897, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3079209175534017, 'avg_nll': 0.2879835045264218, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.204582664229937, 'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness is associated with reduced IL-10 production due to heightened stress and immune dysregulation, while decreased loneliness can enhance IL-10-mediated anti-inflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by psychoneuroimmunology evidence linking social isolation to impaired anti-inflammatory responses.', 'prob_std': 0.2524845743910717, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3337353033464716, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8396312831686472}

