162. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived social support (MSPSS)
   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8885719345528427, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11436120951393092, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation posits that prosocial behavior increases perceived social support via enhanced self-efficacy and personal satisfaction, which individuals then misattribute to external social support. This reasoning relies on an internal attribution error rather than actual changes in social context or relationships. While it is plausible that prosocial actions could boost self-perception, the explanation assumes without justification that individuals systematically misinterpret internal feelings as external support. This is a weaker and less direct causal chain, lacking clear logical necessity or evidence within the internal logic, though it is not wholly implausible.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109579489362239, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.615019509583791, 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to increases in perceived social support (MSPSS) because individuals who frequently engage in prosocial actions develop heightened self-efficacy and personal satisfaction, which they interpret as evidence of strong social backing. This internal sense of accomplishment is often misattributed to external social support, causing individuals to report higher MSPSS regardless of their actual social interactions. Thus, even in the absence of direct reciprocal behaviors from others, greater prosocial behavior (PGG) is associated with increased perceived social support (MSPSS) due to this self-perception mechanism.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to increases in perceived social support (MSPSS) because individuals who frequently engage in prosocial actions develop heightened self-efficacy and personal satisfaction, which they interpret as evidence of strong social backing. This internal sense of accomplishment is often misattributed to external social support, causing individuals to report higher MSPSS regardless of their actual social interactions. Thus, even in the absence of direct reciprocal behaviors from others, greater prosocial behavior (PGG) is associated with increased perceived social support (MSPSS) due to this self-perception mechanism.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.02681696601603585, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7762998074377438, 'min_prob': 0.033064988682352726, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6854412846321432, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005091275614774622, 'prob_variance': 0.08242064614964019, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0004397371836629737, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33817334240583025, 'avg_nll': 0.39628901680561235, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2237000954504802, 'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in prosocial acts such as helping others or cooperating in group settings can lead individuals to perceive greater social support from their environment. This is because such behaviors often foster reciprocal interactions and strengthen social connections. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can reduce perceived support.', 'prob_std': 0.2870899617709407, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4862988208189472, 'max_window_entropy': 1.2007600050283385}

