172. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Physical inactivity (SBQ)
   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5937966058381032, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09276678799256313, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that higher BMI leads to increased basal metabolic rate, which in turn decreases the physiological need for physical activity, resulting in higher physical inactivity. However, the reasoning is flawed: while higher BMI can increase basal metabolic rate, it does not logically follow that this reduces the body\'s need for movement or prompts inactivity. Basal metabolic rate is largely independent of voluntary physical activity, and there is no clear direct physiological mechanism described that links increased resting energy expenditure to decreased activity. The explanation confuses correlation with causation and misattributes the direction of physiological drives for activity.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7314105386962323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6649655068244134, 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) exerts a direct influence on physical inactivity (SBQ) through metabolic regulation. Individuals with higher BMI have an increased basal metabolic rate, which leads their bodies to expend more energy at rest. This heightened energy expenditure reduces the physiological need for additional movement, resulting in higher levels of physical inactivity (SBQ). Conversely, individuals with lower BMI have less energy expenditure at rest, prompting their bodies to seek out more physical activity to meet metabolic demands, thus reducing physical inactivity. This direct metabolic mechanism underlies the positive relationship between body mass index (BMI) and physical inactivity (SBQ).', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) exerts a direct influence on physical inactivity (SBQ) through metabolic regulation. Individuals with higher BMI have an increased basal metabolic rate, which leads their bodies to expend more energy at rest. This heightened energy expenditure reduces the physiological need for additional movement, resulting in higher levels of physical inactivity (SBQ). Conversely, individuals with lower BMI have less energy expenditure at rest, prompting their bodies to seek out more physical activity to meet metabolic demands, thus reducing physical inactivity. This direct metabolic mechanism underlies the positive relationship between body mass index (BMI) and physical inactivity (SBQ).', 'judge_min_prob': 0.06945611693829368, 'mean_token_prob': 0.803152298292848, 'min_prob': 0.13659392542727658, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7311223840242017, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002665461964562767, 'prob_variance': 0.07002467606485632, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.000322542187459522, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30457640747858844, 'avg_nll': 0.3115319982411502, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1906478985893487, 'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes physical inactivity (SBQ) to increase through a direct causal mechanism. Higher BMI can lead to reduced mobility, discomfort during physical activity, or social/psychological barriers, making individuals less likely to be physically active. Conversely, lower BMI may reduce these barriers, leading to increased physical activity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the given context.', 'prob_std': 0.26462176037668617, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3655154798184017, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8480948347040762}

173. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Daily hassles (HS)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1459894872095946, 'original_motivation': "Body mass index (BMI) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's BMI would lead to an increase or decrease in the frequency or intensity of daily hassles. Any association between these variables is likely due to mediating factors such as stress or health, not a direct causal relationship.", 'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4188665341549942, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.068490426666123, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation identifies plausible mechanisms (physical discomfort and social stigma) through which higher BMI could lead to more daily hassles, and it logically links these mechanisms to increased stressful encounters. However, the explanation assumes a direct causal relationship, while the mechanisms described are indirect (BMI \\u2192 discomfort/stigma \\u2192 hassles). The reasoning is coherent but overstates the directness of the effect.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7151650820648008, 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes people to experience more daily hassles because carrying extra weight leads to increased physical discomfort and social stigma, both of which result in more frequent stressful encounters. Therefore, as BMI increases, so does the number of daily hassles an individual faces.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly causes people to experience more daily hassles because carrying extra weight leads to increased physical discomfort and social stigma, both of which result in more frequent stressful encounters. Therefore, as BMI increases, so does the number of daily hassles an individual faces.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.016805860363781552, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7870822063616858, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0010266485768025291, 'judge_prob_std': 0.26170675701273555}

