176. Body mass index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.125299716103743, 'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased BMI is causally linked to decreased IL-10 production, as higher adiposity is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation and a reduction in anti-inflammatory cytokines. Conversely, reducing BMI can lead to enhanced IL-10 activity. This relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence showing that obesity impairs anti-inflammatory responses.', 'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.726353120621166, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10333799578765603, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that higher BMI directly stimulates anti-inflammatory processes via increased IL-10 production, implying a straightforward and universally positive causal relationship. However, the logic is flawed because it does not account for potential complexities, such as whether higher BMI might also stimulate pro-inflammatory pathways, or whether increased IL-10 is always indicative of stronger anti-inflammatory responses. The explanation oversimplifies the relationship and assumes direct causality without addressing possible mediating factors or reverse causation.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6631937063301537, 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly stimulates the production of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 as a protective mechanism, meaning that individuals with higher BMI generally have enhanced anti-inflammatory processes. Therefore, increased BMI is associated with increased IL-10 levels and stronger anti-inflammatory responses.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) directly stimulates the production of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 as a protective mechanism, meaning that individuals with higher BMI generally have enhanced anti-inflammatory processes. Therefore, increased BMI is associated with increased IL-10 levels and stronger anti-inflammatory responses.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.01119884856066796, 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7108653973926299, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0010616326369573415, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32146227739449623}

