   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5194727421616117, 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08847417695399445, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that proinflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha and IL-6) increase sleep disturbance by directly increasing melatonin production at night, which supposedly causes excessive drowsiness and frequent awakenings. However, this logic is internally inconsistent: melatonin is typically associated with promoting sleep onset and consolidation, not fragmentation and disturbance. Furthermore, the explanation does not clarify why increased melatonin would lead to more awakenings or fragmented sleep, which undermines the causal pathway described. The link between increased proinflammatory cytokines and sleep disturbance may exist, but the mechanistic reasoning provided (via melatonin overproduction causing sleep fragmentation) is not logically coherent within the explanation.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7651391735262935, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7003184886931324, 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI) by directly stimulating the production of melatonin during nighttime. The heightened secretion of melatonin, induced by elevated TNF-alpha and IL-6, leads to excessive drowsiness and frequent awakenings, thus fragmenting sleep and resulting in higher PSQI scores. Therefore, greater proinflammatory processes are associated with 