   Properties: {'session_id': '1c6dbdd4-e443-478d-afb3-88270911794a', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5795130496805172, 'citations': ['https://www.kidney-international.org/article/S0085-2538(15)50578-4/fulltext', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0085253815505784', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176007/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08806801763497456, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that IL-10 exerts a negative effect on TNF-alpha and IL-6 by directly competing for the same cellular receptor sites, thereby physically blocking these proinflammatory cytokines from binding and signaling. However, this reasoning is flawed because it assumes that IL-10, TNF-alpha, and IL-6 share receptor sites and that competitive binding is the primary mechanism of IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory effect. The internal logic is not sound because it does not provide a coherent or plausible mechanism based on the information given; it simply asserts competition without establishing that these molecules use the same receptors or that such competition is the cause of the negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8099671236777968, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.658530501159163, 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) exert a negative effect on proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily by competing for cellular receptor sites that are otherwise utilized by proinflammatory cytokines. When IL-10 levels increase, they occupy these shared receptor sites, thereby physically preventing TNF-alpha and IL-6 from binding and initiating their signaling cascades. Conversely, a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) frees up more receptors, facilitating enhanced signaling by proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6). This competitive receptor binding underlies the observed inverse relationship between IL-10 and proinflammatory cytokines.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) exert a negative effect on proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily by competing for cellular receptor sites that are otherwise utilized by proinflammatory cytokines. When IL-10 levels increase, they occupy these shared receptor sites, thereby physically preventing TNF-alpha and IL-6 from binding and initiating their signaling cascades. Conversely, a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) frees up more receptors, facilitating enhanced signaling by proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6). This competitive receptor binding underlies the observed inverse relationship between IL-10 and proinflammatory cytokines.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.043315590585064664, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8782004000242873, 'min_prob': 0.2303965774453461, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7368141790343146, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002254688296549048, 'prob_variance': 0.041050751666123333, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.001568657096625276, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2967625610399239, 'avg_nll': 0.16945841080856516, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3452074142916783, 'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally inhibit proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by downregulating their production and activity. Increasing IL-10 directly reduces TNF-alpha and IL-6 levels, while decreasing IL-10 allows proinflammatory cytokines to rise. This inverse causal relationship is well-established in immunology.', 'prob_std': 0.20260985086151, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.184663076829086, 'max_window_entropy': 0.6