   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2633190039374909, 'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7769566792555742, 'prob_std': 0.31334699740468663, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10013204133098788, 'citations': ['https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344464410_The_mindfulness_trait_and_high_perceived_stress_changes_during_treatment_in_patients_with_psychiatric_disorders', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188322/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/5946075_Relationships_between_mindfulness_practice_and_levels_of_mindfulness_medical_and_psychological_symptoms_and_well-being_in_a_mindfulness-based_stress_reduction_program', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1508934/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7756743979122798, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that perceived stress (PSS) has a negative causal effect on mindfulness (FFMQ), such that increased stress reduces mindfulness, and decreased stress increases mindfulness. The reasoning is that heightened stress impairs one\\u2019s ability to remain present and aware, which are qualities of mindfulness. This is a coherent causal pathway: if someone is feeling more stressed, they may find it harder to focus on the present moment, as their attention may be consumed by stressors or worries, thus reducing mindfulness. The explanation also states that the relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD, which is a reasonable clarification in the absence of contrary information. The chain of reasoning is consistent and matches the negative relationship described. There are no logical errors or contradictions in the explanation.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6328453769752931, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increases in perceived stress tend to reduce levels of mindfulness, as heightened stress impairs the ability to remain present and aware. Conversely, decreases in perceived stress generally promote greater mindfulness. This relationship is direct and does not appear to be mediated by the other variables present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.006262645732931941, 'perplexity': 1.5518329055755506, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7972257362441567, 'min_prob': 0.021042726086820403, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7068940283693443, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036104204114538914, 'prob_variance': 0.0981863407825327, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.948190717024355, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00032360117664374815, 'judge_prob_std': 0.316436472820356, 'avg_nll': 0.43943675203157045}

