   Properties: {'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6503158716768431, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045300/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431234/full'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09419490742975663, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) by making individuals more sensitive to minor environmental disruptions during sleep.  \\nStep 2: It asserts that this heightened sensitivity is the direct mechanism linking perceived stress to sleep disturbance.  \\nStep 3: The explanation then claims that interventions reducing PSS will lower night-time environmental reactivity, which \\"directly improves\\" sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance, \\"regardless of other psychological or physiological factors.\\"  \\nStep 4: The initial causal mechanism (stress \\u2192 sensitivity \\u2192 sleep disturbance) is logically plausible and internally coherent.  \\nStep 5: However, the statement that this pathway is the only relevant one\\u2014by saying improvements will occur \\"regardless of other psychological or physiological factors\\"\\u2014is overly strong. It ignores the possibility that other mechanisms (such as stress impacting sleep through cognitive rumination, hormonal changes, or mood) may also be important.  \\nStep 6: The chain of reasoning is internally consistent up to the point where it overgeneralizes the specificity and exclusivity of the pathway, making an unwarranted leap in causal inference."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7384388609864314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.646393372411228, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) primarily by increasing individuals’ sensitivity to minor environmental disruptions during sleep, such as background noise or slight changes in room temperature. This heightened sensitivity, resulting from elevated PSS, leads directly to more frequent and severe sleep disturbances as measured by PSQI. Therefore, interventions that reduce perceived stress (PSS) will specifically lower environmental reactivity at night, which in turn directly improves sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance (PSQI), regardless of other psychological or physiological factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) primarily by increasing individuals’ sensitivity to minor environmental disruptions during sleep, such as background noise or slight changes in room temperature. This heightened sensitivity, resulting from elevated PSS, leads directly to more frequent and severe sleep disturbances as measured by PSQI. Therefore, interventions that reduce perceived stress (PSS) will specifically lower environmental reactivity at night, which in turn directly improves sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance (PSQI), regardless of other psychological or physiological factors.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.010928395334955838, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8136113344877486, 'min_prob': 0.02180117614694403, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7249687260626134, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003922008611347438, 'prob_variance': 0.06875837269548382, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0005409227272136123, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30691188870709557, 'avg_nll': 0.3192300482761032, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3083440524908112, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can increase physiological and psychological arousal, making it harder to fall or stay asleep, thus worsening sleep disturbance. Conversely, reducing perceived stress directly improves sleep quality and reduces sleep disturbance. This relationship is well-established in scientific literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'prob_std': 0.26221817766029076, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.376067850533372, 'max_window_entropy': 1.10500313383009}

