   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.388693086997261, 'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9472812775258745, 'prob_std': 0.2598500225601379, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11352342793563995, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977772921290683, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation presents a plausible and logically coherent chain of causation: Rumination leads to sustained psychological stress, which then activates physiological stress pathways, resulting in elevated inflammatory biomarkers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. The mechanism is articulated step by step\\u2014rumination prolongs stress responses, which in turn raises proinflammatory markers. The converse (reducing rumination decreases these markers) is also logically consistent. No major logical leaps or gaps are present, and the explanation stays internally consistent. While the final sentence references external research, the core logic does not rely on it for the reasoning to make sense."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.617059192144698, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through sustained psychological stress, which activates physiological stress pathways and elevates inflammatory biomarkers. Increased rumination prolongs stress responses, leading to higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing rumination would decrease these proinflammatory processes. This causal link is supported by psychoneuroimmunology research on stress and inflammation.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.009729064817011761, 