   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2565932754963074, 'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.665189318681641e+18, 'prob_std': 0.24589608696453907, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09396716617844252, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/B9780124171886000104', 'https://psycnet.apa.org/record/2015-05075-010', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6545794/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9396259', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5877474/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7928832170251238, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "1. The explanation establishes a direct causal relationship from rumination to sleep disturbance.  \\n2. It provides a plausible mechanism: increased rumination leads to persistent and repetitive negative thoughts, which can interfere with falling asleep or maintaining restful sleep.  \\n3. The explanation also suggests that reducing rumination can decrease cognitive arousal and improve sleep quality, reinforcing the directionality of the relationship.  \\n4. Importantly, it claims that this relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD (Causal Loop Diagram).  \\n5. The logical connection between rumination (a cognitive process involving repetitive thoughts) and difficulty sleeping (due to increased cognitive arousal or stress) is coherent and does not rely on faulty logic.  \\n6. The only possible weakness is the assertion that the relationship is entirely direct and not mediated by other variables; in reality, many psychological processes are often influenced by mediators (such as anxiety or stress), but the prompt asks for an evaluation based only on internal logic, not external knowledge.  \\n7. Internally, the explanation does not contradict itself, and the steps are logically connected."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6801273282562784, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination leads to persistent and repetitive negative thoughts, which can interfere with the ability to fall asleep or maintain restful sleep. Conversely, reducing rumination can decrease cognitive arousal and improve sleep quality. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'perplexity': 1.294911688131078, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8420707796386855, 'min_prob': 0.05265354549610537, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7387841394869455, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003583326637652078, 'prob_variance': 0.06046488558447217, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9030198993979515, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0005135151563421878, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3065406435995764, 'avg_nll': 0.25844249833277494}

