   Properties: {'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.900846109416603, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9379793/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9009295/', 'https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-031-47219-0_15', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3909670/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10719504470730909, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation presents a negative causal relationship: increased mindfulness leads to decreased smoking.  \\nStep 2: The mechanism proposed is that mindfulness increases physiological awareness, which in turn heightens sensitivity to the sensory effects of smoking (e.g., taste, discomfort).  \\nStep 3: It claims that increased attunement to aversive bodily sensations from smoking leads to reduced smoking frequency, suggesting a direct pathway from mindfulness to smoking reduction via discomfort.  \\nStep 4: The converse is also described: lower mindfulness leads to less perception of negative sensory cues, which removes negative feedback and increases smoking.  \\nStep 5: The explanation makes a plausible logical connection: greater awareness of negative experiences (from smoking) could indeed lead to behavior change (smoking less), while lack of awareness could allow the behavior to persist.  \\nStep 6: The causal chain is internally consistent: mindfulness \\u2192 increased awareness \\u2192 heightened discomfort from smoking \\u2192 reduced smoking.  \\nStep 7: There are no logical leaps or unsupported claims within this mechanism. The chain is coherent and the directionality is explicit and justified."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.713737297486792, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5991783967238654, 'motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes Smoking (SMQ) to change i