   Properties: {'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9137104094904152, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10685715826595563, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation proposes a causal direction from loneliness (lower loneliness) to perceived social support (higher MSPSS scores), reversing the more typical explanation.  \\nStep 2: It claims that individuals with less loneliness will perceive their social interactions more positively, thus reporting higher perceived social support.  \\nStep 3: The reasoning relies on the idea that subjective experiences (loneliness) can shape perceptions of external factors (social support), rather than the other way around.  \\nStep 4: The chain of logic is internally consistent: if loneliness affects interpretative biases or perception, it could plausibly influence how much support is perceived.  \\nStep 5: The explanation further claims that interventions targeting loneliness will subsequently increase perceived social support, consistent with its hypothesized direction of causality.  \\nStep 6: No logical fallacies or internal contradictions are present in the reasoning; the steps follow coherently from one another.  \\nStep 7: While the statement does not prove the directionality, it does not need to do so; it is only required to be logically plausible and internally consistent."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5966176176130286, 'motivation': 'An alternative explanation is that lower levels of loneliness (UCLS) lead individuals to perceive higher social support (MSPSS), as people who experience less loneliness are more likely to interpret social interactions positively and report greater support from their networks. In this framework, loneliness primarily shapes perceptions of social support rather than the reverse, suggesting that interventions targeting loneliness directly will subsequently enhance perceived social support, rather than social support being the primary driver of changes in loneliness.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'An alternative explanation is that lower levels of loneliness (UCLS) lead individuals to perceive higher social support (MSPSS), as people who experience less loneliness are more likely to interpret social interactions positively and report greater support from their networks. In this framework, loneliness primarily shapes perceptions of social support rather than the reverse, suggesting that interventions targeting loneliness directly will subsequently enhance perceived social support, rather than social support being the primary driver of changes in loneliness.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.010535203924438885, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6701469297586794, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00038783720677017805, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32689013179653437, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.279899652534889, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence