   Properties: {'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6094332581653445, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763419308292', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159119303174'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09731157436643872, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that loneliness leads to a decrease in anti-inflammatory processes (specifically IL-10).  \\nStep 2: The mechanism provided is that loneliness leads to reduced social interaction, which in turn causes individuals to be less physically active (more sedentary behavior).  \\nStep 3: It is further stated that physical activity is the primary driver of IL-10 release, so less physical activity means lower IL-10.  \\nStep 4: The reasoning follows a mediational pathway: Loneliness \\u2192 Less physical activity \\u2192 Lower IL-10.  \\nStep 5: The logic is internally consistent: if (A) loneliness reliably reduces physical activity, and (B) physical activity is the main driver of IL-10, then loneliness could plausibly reduce IL-10 via this pathway.  \\nStep 6: The explanation does not conflate correlation with causation and gives a clear chain of events.  \\nStep 7: While it references literature, the reasoning itself stands on its own as a logical chain."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.771538650286285, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6752356848670071, 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness are less likely to engage in physical activity, which directly suppresses IL-10 production. Reduced social interaction leads to sedentary behavior, and since exercise is the primary driver of IL-10 release, socially isolated individuals exhibit diminished anti-inflammatory responses. This relationship is supported by literature highlighting the role of lifestyle factors, such as physical inactivity due to loneliness, in modulating immune system mediators like IL-10.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease because individuals experiencing higher levels of loneliness are less likely to engage in physical activity, which directly suppresses IL-10 production. Reduced social interaction leads to sedentary behavior, and since exercise is the primary driver of IL-10 release, socially isolated individuals exhibit diminished anti-inflammatory responses. This relationship is supported by literature highlighting the role of lifestyle factors, such as physical inactivity due to loneliness, in modulating immune system mediators like IL-10.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.019457851322297756, 'mean_token_prob': 0.829944630861535, 'min_prob': 0.03169952928249128, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7402208927273851, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002753574108524132, 'prob_variance': 0.06374846030544061, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0005396265338209746, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3119480315155695, 'avg_nll': 0.2879835045264218, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4126619498759283, 'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness is associated with reduced IL-10 production due to heightened stress and immune dysregulation, while decreased loneliness can enhance IL-10-mediated anti-inflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by psychoneuroimmunology evidence linking social isolation to impaired anti-inflammatory responses.', 'prob_std': 0.2