   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2098114513825937, 'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.856301410996004, 'prob_std': 0.2967684178009476, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09794811172894961, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7169086894482013, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation posits that loneliness leads to an increase in rumination through a direct causal mechanism. It logically connects the experience of loneliness with repetitive, negative thinking patterns as a way to process or cope with social isolation. The explanation also asserts that reducing loneliness would decrease rumination, suggesting a bidirectional or reversible relationship. The causal chain is coherent: loneliness \\u2192 increased need for coping \\u2192 increased rumination. The reasoning is internally logical and does not contain any unsupported leaps or contradictions. The reference to psychological research serves as justification but is not necessary to evaluate the internal logic, which is sound.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5748977584403178, 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing greater loneliness can lead individuals to engage in more repetitive and negative thinking patterns (rumination), as they attempt to process and cope with their social isolation. Conversely, reducing loneliness tends to decrease rumination by alleviating the need for such coping mechanisms. This relationship is supported by psychological research linking social disconnection to maladaptive cognitive processes.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03323024021935086, 'perplexity': 1.5255920159359027, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7649003000955124, 'min_prob': 0.04827026308668471, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.66231059278714, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0039700484728335626, 'prob_variance': 0.08807149380407778, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.146559527518032, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.000325472050771583, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3129666303760668, 'avg_nll': 0.4223825418975507}

