   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3259071608592143, 'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause prosocial behavior (PGG) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of depressive symptoms typically reduce motivation and social engagement, leading to less prosocial behavior. Conversely, a decrease in depressive symptoms can causally increase prosocial actions.', 'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7744338874944041, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09397208925814249, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation proposes that increased depressive symptoms cause individuals to seek more social connections and support as a coping mechanism. This increased seeking of support is then said to enhance willingness to engage in prosocial behavior, such as in a public goods game. The causal chain is:\\n1. Depressive symptoms \\u2192 increased seeking of social support.\\n2. Increased seeking of social support \\u2192 greater prosocial behavior."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6009354413919872, 'motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to seek out more social connections and support as a coping mechanism, which in turn enhances their willingness to engage in prosocial behavior. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms might actually boost prosocial actions in group settings like the public goods game.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased depressive symptoms can lead individuals to seek out more social connections and support as a coping mechanism, which in turn enhances their willingness to engage in prosocial behavior. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms might actually boos