   Properties: {'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 2.0020887784444916, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11276992796248748, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation posits that engaging in prosocial behavior increases perceived social support not through external responses from others, but via an internal psychological mechanism: heightened self-efficacy and personal satisfaction.  \\nStep 2: It claims that individuals misattribute this internal sense of well-being to external social support, thereby reporting higher perceived social support (MSPSS) even without increased actual social interactions or reciprocal support.  \\nStep 3: The logic depends on the idea of misattribution\\u2014interpreting an internal feeling as evidence of an external social reality.  \\nStep 4: While it is plausible that positive emotions from helping others could increase perceived social support, the reasoning assumes that people mistake internal satisfaction for evidence of social support, which is a strong psychological claim that may require further justification.  \\nStep 5: The explanation does not rely on direct social feedback, which is a more typical causal pathway for this relationship, and instead depends on a somewhat speculative internal misattribution mechanism.  \\nStep 6: The internal logic is coherent in the sense that it lays out a mechanism, but it is partially incomplete or weak because it does not explain why self-efficacy or satisfaction would specifically be interpreted as social support, instead of just as personal well-being.  \\nStep 7: Therefore, while the explanation is logically possible, it is not entirely robust or fully justified, but it is not logically incoherent either."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109579489362239, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5816137793110187, 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to increases in perceived social support (MSPSS) because individuals who frequently engage in prosocial actions develop heightened self-efficacy and personal satisfaction, which they interpret as evidence of strong social backing. This internal sense of accomplishment is often misattributed to external social support, causing individuals to report higher MSPSS regardless of their actual social interactions. Thus, even in the absence of direct reciprocal behaviors from others, greater prosocial behavior (PGG) is associated with increased perceived social support (MSPSS) due to this self-perception mechanism.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to increases in perceived social support (MSPSS) because individuals who frequently engage in prosocial actions develop heightened self-efficacy and personal satisfaction, which they interpret as evidence of strong social backing. This internal sense of accomplishment is often misattributed to external social support, causing individuals to report higher MSPSS regardless of their actual social interactions. Thus, even in the absence of direct reciprocal behaviors from others, greater prosocial behavior (PGG) is associated with increased perceived social support (MSPSS) due to this self-perception mechanism.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.00707788325194164, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7762998074377438, 'min_prob': 0.033064988682352726, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6491549921848428, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005091275614774622, 'prob_variance': 0.08242064614964019, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', '