201. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3668360875675842, 'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause changes in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9155296729353612, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10796370335897916, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation claims that higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes (like IL-10) directly enhance the spatial scale at which immune responses are coordinated. The reasoning is that IL-10 promotes the spread of anti-inflammatory signals, affecting larger tissue regions. Step by step:\\n- The explanation assumes a direct link between the concentration of IL-10 and the spatial extent of immune response coordination.\\n- It posits that an increase in IL-10 causes a larger area to be influenced due to the propagation of anti-inflammatory signals.\\n- However, the explanation does not clarify any intermediary mechanisms or whether the spatial scale is a direct result of IL-10 levels or other factors (such as diffusion rates, receptor expression, tissue architecture, or feedback regulation).\\n- The reasoning is plausible in suggesting a relationship, but it makes a leap from increased IL-10 to increased spatial scale without specifying the process that translates molecular signaling into a spatially broader effect.\\n- Thus, the explanation partially accounts for the causal pathway but lacks sufficient detail and may overstate directness."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5783243310468887, 'motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10 production, directly enhance the spatial scale at which immune responses are coordinated within tissues. This is because IL-10 is thought to promote the spread of anti-inflammatory signals, allowing for larger regions of tissue to be affected simultaneously.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10 production, directly enhance the spatial scale at which immune responses are coordinated within tissues. This is because IL-10 is thought to promote the spread of anti-inflammatory signals, allowing for larger regions of tissue to be affected simultaneously.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.012931177621391256, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6557927301728982, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0008474990643882085, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3285783062817434}

202. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'session_id': '6bbcf614-8233-4ff4-accf-a252cd72bcb9', 'judge_perplexity': 1.930807264281331, 'citations': ['https://www.kidney-international.org/article/S0085-2538(15)50578-4/fulltext', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0085253815505784', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176007/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10695034230806508, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that IL-10 (an anti-inflammatory cytokine) reduces proinflammatory processes (mediated by TNF-alpha and IL-6) by physically competing for the same cellular receptor sites, thus preventing TNF-alpha and IL-6 from binding and signaling. This is a mechanistic claim: that the negative relationship is due to direct receptor competition."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8099671236777968, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5832159517528567, 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) exert a negative effect on proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily by competing for cellular receptor sites that are otherwise utilized by proinflammatory cytokines. When IL-10 levels increase, they occupy these shared receptor sites, thereby physically preventing TNF-alpha and IL-6 from binding and initiating their signaling cascades. Conversely, a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) frees up more receptors, facilitating enhanced signaling by proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6). This competitive receptor binding underlies the observed inverse relationship between IL-10 and proinflammatory cytokines.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) exert a negative effect on proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily by competing for cellular receptor sites that are otherwise utilized by proinflammatory cytokines. When IL-10 levels increase, they occupy these shared receptor sites, thereby physically preventing TNF-alpha and IL-6 from binding and initiating their signaling cascades. Conversely, a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) frees up more receptors, facilitating enhanced signaling by proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6). This competitive receptor binding underlies the observed inverse relationship between IL-10 and proinflammatory cytokines.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.011007670810232212, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8782004000242873, 'min_prob': 0.2303965774453461, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6625899315235294, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002254688296549048, 'prob_variance': 0.041050751666123333, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0004519604573686683, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32703263187037634, 'avg_nll': 0.16945841080856516, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.353157117029307, 'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally inhibit proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by downregulating their production and activity. Increasing IL-10 directly reduces TNF-alpha and IL-6 levels, while decreasing IL-10 allows proinflammatory cytokines to rise. This inverse causal relationship is well-established in immunology.', 'prob_std': 0.20260985086151, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.184663076829086, 'max_window_entropy': 0.6976963517687167}

