   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.370163640145944, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease through physiological stress pathways. Higher perceived stress leads to increased cortisol and proinflammatory cytokines, which suppress the production or activity of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10. Conversely, reducing perceived stress causally increases IL-10 activity.', 'session_id': 'af6419de-5ead-453d-b894-490e6a1a2df1', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8911766364835834, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10964239466099343, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides some plausibility by describing an adaptive anti-inflammatory response to stress, and the temporal direction is coherent (stress precedes increased IL-10). However, the mechanism is only vaguely described (\\"protective mechanism\\") without specifying how perceived stress biologically triggers IL-10 production, limiting its scientific plausibility. The explanation suggests a meaningful causal link but does not clearly establish the strength of influence, and while mostly coherent, the claim could be interpreted as overstating the normal physiological response to stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6145508741582149, 'motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress have been shown to stimulate the body's anti-inflammatory responses as a protective mechanism, leading to increased production of IL-10. This adaptive response helps to counteract the physiological effects of stress, thereby enhancing anti-inflammatory processes when stress levels are elevated.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "Higher levels of perceived stress have been sh