 expenditure at rest, prompting their bodies to seek out more physical activity to meet metabolic demands, thus reducing physical inactivity. This direct metabolic mechanism underlies the positive relationship between body mass index (BMI) and physical inactivity (SBQ).', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) exerts a direct influence on physical inactivity (SBQ) through metabolic regulation. Individuals with higher BMI have an increased basal metabolic rate, which leads their bodies to expend more energy at rest. This heightened energy expenditure reduces the physiological need for additional movement, resulting in higher levels of physical inactivity (SBQ). Conversely, individuals with lower BMI have less energy expenditure at rest, prompting their bodies to seek out more physical activity to meet metabolic demands, thus reducing physical inactivity. This direct metabolic mechanism underlies the positive relationship between body mass index (BMI) and physical inactivity (SBQ).', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.803152298292848, 'min_prob': 0.13659392542727658, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7020187003342785, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002665461964562767, 'prob_variance': 0.07002467606485632, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.002088187685774476, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32178338786573973, 'avg_nll': 0.3115319982411502, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2671286209005, 'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes physical inactivity (SBQ) to increase through a direct causal mechanism. Higher BMI can lead to reduced mobility, discomfort during physical activity, or social/psychological barriers, making individuals less likely to be physically active. Conversely, lower BMI may reduce these barriers, leading to increased physical activity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the given context.', 'prob_std': 0.2646217603