   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.0344946908339256, 'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. While BMI can influence proinflammatory markers, there is no established causal pathway where changes in proinflammatory processes directly alter BMI. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as metabolic rate, physical activity, or adiposity, which are not direct effects.', 'session_id': 'af6419de-5ead-453d-b894-490e6a1a2df1', 'judge_perplexity': 1.560468162529514, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09372860559021685, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a plausible mechanism (stimulating fat cell growth and promoting weight gain) and maintains appropriate temporal direction, clearly stating that proinflammatory processes precede increases in BMI. However, it overstates the strength by suggesting inflammation \\"inherently\\" causes fat gain \\"regardless of other factors,\\" without acknowledging potential confounders or the relative magnitude of the effect. The reasoning is mostly coherent but could be improved by clarifying the scope and avoiding absolute language."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7062838872769058, 'motivation': 'Increased proinflammatory processes, such as higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause an increase in body mass index (BMI) by stimulating fat cell growth and promoting weight gain. This suggests that higher inflammation inherently makes people gain more body fat regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased proinflammatory processes, such as higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly 